<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Ratzeburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1236 CE – 1648 CE (approx.) · capital Schönberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Lauenburg church state founded by Henry the Lion among the Polabian Slavs, secularised to Mecklenburg in 1648.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> founded the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a> in 1154 during the conquest and conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Slavic Polabians</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evermod">Evermod</a> as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. The bishops built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque</a> brick cathedral on the island of Ratzeburg and acquired lands east of the lake. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry's</a> fall they ruled these lands as an imperial principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schonberg-mecklenburg">Schönberg</a>, was small and rural, settled by German farmers among the earlier Slavic population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were overshadowed by the dukes of Saxe-Lauenburg, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a> town, and by the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a>. In the 16th century the cathedral chapter turned Lutheran, and Mecklenburg princes held the see as administrators.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 secularised the see as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-ratzeburg">Principality of Ratzeburg</a> and gave it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Duchy of Mecklenburg</a>. From 1701 it belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a> and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-ratzeburg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Ratzeburg" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn/ (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bishopric_of_Ratzeburg_locator_map_(1250).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1236 CE – 1648 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schönberg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Evermod of Ratzeburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Ratzeburg, Hochstift Ratzeburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1442&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-ratzeburg" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Ratzeburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4988 473 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Ratzeburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5308 729L5229 694M5308 729L5291 598M5308 729L5308 729"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5291 598h0M5308 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5308 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5308 729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Schönberg. <a href="/atlas?year=1442&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-ratzeburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
